@basket = https://localhost:7290
@gateway = https://localhost:7260
@identity = https://localhost:7130
@catalog = https://localhost:7232

### Liveness — is the process responsive?
GET {{basket}}/alive

### Readiness — responsive AND can reach SQL Server
GET {{basket}}/health

### OpenAPI document
GET {{basket}}/openapi/v1.json

### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### Every basket route needs a token: the basket is always the caller's own, keyed
### by the sub claim. Run this first, then the rest reuse the token.
### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Sign in as the seeded customer
# @name login
POST {{identity}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "customer@shoplite.local",
  "password": "Passw0rd!"
}

@token = {{login.response.body.token}}

### Find a product to add
# @name products
GET {{catalog}}/products?page=1&pageSize=5
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

@productId = {{products.response.body.items[0].id}}

### Get the basket — returns an empty basket, not 404, when nothing was ever added
GET {{basket}}/basket
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Add an item. Basket calls Catalog for the name and price, then snapshots them.
POST {{basket}}/basket/items
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 2
}

### Add the same item again — this INCREMENTS the quantity.
### Send it twice and the quantity goes up twice: that is the bug an
### Idempotency-Key exists to prevent.
POST {{basket}}/basket/items
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 2
}

### Now with an Idempotency-Key. Send this one as many times as you like —
### the quantity changes once. Change the key to apply it again.
POST {{basket}}/basket/items
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: try-me-0001
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 2
}

### Set an absolute quantity. Naturally idempotent, so it needs no key —
### repeat it and the answer never changes. Does not call Catalog.
PUT {{basket}}/basket/items/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quantity": 3
}

### Quantity 0 is rejected: 400 with validation details
PUT {{basket}}/basket/items/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quantity": 0
}

### A product Catalog does not have: 404, not 503 — Catalog answered, it just said no
POST {{basket}}/basket/items
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000ff",
  "quantity": 1
}

### Remove one line
DELETE {{basket}}/basket/items/{{productId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Empty the basket. Succeeds whether or not it had anything in it.
DELETE {{basket}}/basket
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### No token at all: 401
GET {{basket}}/basket

### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### Through the gateway. Note the path is unchanged — unlike /catalog and
### /identity, the /basket prefix is not stripped, because Basket's own resource
### is already /basket. See ADR 0006.
### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### The same basket, via the gateway
GET {{gateway}}/basket
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### To watch the resilience pipeline: stop Catalog in the Aspire dashboard, then
### run the add below. The first call takes seconds while retries time out; once
### the circuit opens, the rest return in milliseconds with priceFromCache true.
### A product Basket has never priced returns 503 instead of a guess.
### ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Add a previously-priced product while Catalog is down
POST {{basket}}/basket/items
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 1
}
